      "id": 1,
      "text": "Little America was named after the Antarctic base camp established by polar explorer Richard E. Byrd in 1928.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_America%2C_Wyoming"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first Little America opened in 1934 as a remote outpost for travelers on the windswept plains of western Wyoming.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://wyshs.org/little-america-wy"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Robert Earl Holding and his wife Carol expanded the site from a struggling station into a larger business empire.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://wyshs.org/historic-little-america"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A brutal winter storm in 1979 trapped over 500 people at the Little America station in Wyoming.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://wyshs.org/historic-little-america"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The owners of Little America purchased the Sinclair Oil Corporation in 1976 in one of the most significant business moves in Wyoming history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://wyshs.org/historic-little-america"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "For decades, Little America kept its prices famously low, offering services for a nickel or a dime in the early days.",
